content.text:
## Root cause

`MessageDispatcher` had two non-virtual overloads: `route(const Message&)` and `route(const PriorityMessage&)`. C++ overload resolution for non-virtual functions is resolved **statically**, based on the declared (compile-time) type of the expression — not the runtime type of the object.

In `test_001.cpp`, messages are stored as `std::vector<const Message*>`, so even a `PriorityMessage` object is accessed through a `const Message*`. Dereferencing gives a `const Message&`, so `dispatcher.route(*msg)` always binds to `route(const Message&)`, which unconditionally enqueued to `standard_queue_` — regardless of actual priority.

## Fix

`src/dispatcher/message_dispatcher.cpp:12-24` now bases the routing decision on the `priority()` field stored on the base `Message` class (available regardless of static type) instead of relying on which overload happened to get picked:

```cpp
void MessageDispatcher::route(const Message& message) {
    if (message.payload().empty()) {
        dlq_.enqueue(message);
        return;
    }
    if (message.priority() == MessagePriority::CRITICAL || message.priority() == MessagePriority::HIGH) {
        priority_queue_.enqueue(message);
    } else {
        standard_queue_.enqueue(message);
    }
}

void MessageDispatcher::route(const PriorityMessage& message) {
    route(static_cast<const Message&>(message));
}
```

This makes routing correct no matter which static type is used to call it, satisfies empty-payload → DLQ, CRITICAL/HIGH → priority queue, LOW/NORMAL → standard queue. Verified by compiling and running `test_001.cpp` with clang++ — output: `SUCCESS`.
